It acts like a register. So you can do binding prefetches (read Chris Metcalf's paper on
prefetches and non blocking loads)

So load [mainmem, l1(offset)] at this point l1(offset) acts like a register and the
compiler can replace address references with this. Duh!!

So you don't suffer from silly cache associativity problems and can control your own
prefetching logic!!

Spatial locality can be sw controlled too. Meaning if your access pattern is fixed
but not sequential then you can take a hint and fetch the pattern and this pattern
can be changed new ones added without changing Silicon. Kind of similar to our
texture memory yes!


